<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khaz'al ibn Jabir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1863–1936</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sheikh Khazal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khaz'al ibn Jabir was the Arab sheikh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Mohammerah</a>, modern Khorramshahr, who from 1897 ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> as a semi-independent prince under nominal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. A close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, he leased the land at Abadan on which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bp">Anglo-Persian Oil Company</a> built its refinery. After he resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan</a>'s centralisation in 1924, he was arrested in 1925 and held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khazal-ibn-jabir.jpg" alt="Khaz'al ibn Jabir" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lieutenant-General_H.H_Shaikh_Khaz%E2%80%99al_Khan_ibn_Haji_Jabir_Khan,_Sardar-i-Aqdas,_Amir_of_Mohammerah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
